# Shell scripts: Git writes them with LF on checkout, whatever core.autocrlf says. A Windows
# checkout's copies also run or get read under Linux: Docker mounts the Postgres init script into
# its container, Helm inlines the chart's wait-for-db.sh into the pod, and the runbook's
# shellcheck command lints the scripts in scripts/ and the charts' files/ in a container.
# Under Linux each line of a CRLF copy ends in a stray CR. A shebang line (`#!/bin/bash\r`)
# then names an interpreter that doesn't exist, so the kernel can't run the script (bash reports
# "cannot execute: required file not found"). A shell that runs the script's text keeps each CR
# as part of the line's last word.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Dockerfile and .dockerignore: Git writes them with LF on checkout, whatever core.autocrlf says,
# so a build from a Windows checkout reads the same bytes as CI's. A heredoc in a RUN step of a
# CRLF Dockerfile would hand each of its lines to the Linux shell with a trailing CR.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
